Q1. What is a Cursor in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

A cursor is a pointer to the context area that stores the result set
of an SQL statement.

It allows PL/SQL to process query results one row at a time.

Key Points

• Pointer to the context area.
• Used for row-by-row processing.
• Processes query results.
• Mainly used with SELECT statements returning multiple rows.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need a Cursor?

Interview Ready Answer

A cursor is required when a query returns multiple rows and each row
needs to be processed individually.

SQL retrieves the complete result set, whereas a cursor allows
PL/SQL to process one row at a time.

Uses

• Row-by-row processing.
• Apply business logic on each row.
• Perform calculations.
• Validate each record individually.

======================================================================

Q3. What are the Types of Cursors?

Interview Ready Answer

PL/SQL provides two types of cursors.

1. Implicit Cursor
2. Explicit Cursor

======================================================================

Q4. What is an Implicit Cursor?

Interview Ready Answer

An implicit cursor is automatically created and managed by Oracle
whenever an SQL statement is executed.

Programmer does not create or manage it.

Used With

• INSERT
• UPDATE
• DELETE
• SELECT...INTO (Single Row)

Key Points

• Created automatically.
• Managed by Oracle.
• No OPEN, FETCH or CLOSE required.

======================================================================

Q5. What is an Explicit Cursor?

Interview Ready Answer

An explicit cursor is a cursor created by the programmer to process
multiple rows returned by a SELECT statement.

The programmer is responsible for opening, fetching and closing
the cursor.

Key Points

• Created manually.
• Used for multiple-row queries.
• Supports row-by-row processing.
• Requires OPEN, FETCH and CLOSE.

======================================================================

Q6. Difference between Implicit Cursor and Explicit Cursor.

Interview Ready Answer

Implicit Cursor

• Automatically created by Oracle.
• No programmer control.
• Used for single-row queries and DML statements.
• OPEN, FETCH and CLOSE handled automatically.

------------------------------------------------------------

Explicit Cursor

• Created by programmer.
• Programmer controls execution.
• Used for multiple-row queries.
• Requires OPEN, FETCH and CLOSE statements.

======================================================================

Q7. Why can't SELECT...INTO be used for multiple rows?

Interview Ready Answer

SELECT...INTO is designed to retrieve exactly one row.

If the query returns

• No rows → NO_DATA_FOUND
• More than one row → TOO_MANY_ROWS

Therefore, SELECT...INTO cannot be used for multiple-row queries.

For multiple rows, an Explicit Cursor should be used.

======================================================================

Q8. Why do we use Explicit Cursor?

Interview Ready Answer

Explicit cursors are used when a SELECT statement returns multiple
rows and each row needs to be processed individually.

Advantages

• Processes one row at a time.
• Suitable for business logic.
• Gives programmer complete control.
• Handles multiple-row result sets.

======================================================================

Q9. What are the basic operations performed on an Explicit Cursor?

Interview Ready Answer

An explicit cursor performs four operations.

1. Declare Cursor
2. OPEN Cursor
3. FETCH Rows
4. CLOSE Cursor

Purpose

DECLARE

→ Defines the cursor.

OPEN

→ Executes the query.

FETCH

→ Retrieves one row at a time.

CLOSE

→ Releases cursor resources.

======================================================================

Q10. When should we use Cursor instead of SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

A cursor should be used when row-by-row processing is required.

Use SQL

• Bulk operations.
• Set-based processing.
• Simple data retrieval.

Use Cursor

• Business logic on each row.
• Validation for each record.
• Processing multiple rows individually.

